    "explanation": "To the rider and the driver: this dispute cannot be decided on the record as it stands, so it goes to a human reviewer and the $12.00 in dispute is held — it is neither collected nor refunded. An escalation is terminal and replaces any money ruling.\n\nWhat is established. The trip was longer than the route the platform computed at booking. The GPS trace measures 17.57 km against a 14.0 km optimal route — an excess of 3.57 km, or 25.5% of optimal. POL-RD-01 makes the trace figure govern over the rounded 17.6 km convenience copy on the trip record. That clears the establishment gate (over 20% and over 0.8 km) but not the materiality gate (over 50% or over 8.0 km), so the deviation is established and not material. The rider's profile shows 5 deviation claims in 60 days with 1 substantiated, so a claim below 30% needs corroboration beyond the trace; that is supplied by the driver's own statement that he went by Newton and Kim Seng. The trace itself is clean (100% coverage, no gaps, 10-second sampling), so POL-RD-07 and POL-RD-10(3) do not bite, and POL-RD-04, POL-RD-05 and POL-RD-12 do not move the baseline (no rider instruction, no amended endpoints, not a pooled trip).\n\nThe driver's defence and why it stops the ruling. The driver says the CTE was jammed near Chin Swee on Saturday night and he went by Newton and Kim Seng to avoid it. A traffic justification is a POL-RD-03 matter, and POL-RD-03 requires a traffic advisory that names a road or segment the optimal route uses and carries a validity window covering the trip. The only advisory in the bundle, EV-ADVIS-00107-01, is an empty placeholder: its payload is null, it names no road and carries no window, and it stands at weight 0.0. An exculpatory clause (POL-RD-03) has therefore been invoked against a cited artifact that lacks the fields the clause requires, and POL-RD-10(5) makes escalation mandatory in exactly that situation. The rider is right that this advisory exonerates nothing; that is precisely why the record cannot test the driver's defence, and the answer is a human review rather than a ruling against the driver on a defence the record does not allow anyone to check.\n\nWhat the figures would have been. Had a usable advisory been in the record, the POL-RD-02 arithmetic (METERED fare; surge 1.35 read from fare_breakdown.surge_multiplier, which is where the multiplier lives, not the trip record) would give recomputed_fare = ((3.80 + 0.75 × 14.0 + 0.28 × 24.0) × 1.35) + 0.80 = 29.177, and a refund of charged_total 36.60 − 29.177 = 7.423, rounded up to $7.45 — not the $12.00 claimed. No repeat-deviation pattern is established under POL-RD-06 (deviation_flags_90d of 1 against 619 completed trips; the lifetime prior_deviation_flags counter is context only and was not used), so no full-refund uplift and no conduct referral arise. There is no ERP gantry and no surcharge line, so POL-RD-09 corrects nothing and POL-RD-09(4) billing integrity does not fire. No clause produces a referral here.\n\nWhat would resolve this. The LTA traffic advisory for the CTE for 18 July 2026, roughly 21:00–22:00 SGT, showing whether a closure or congestion advisory was in force and, if so, the shortest lawful alternative distance. With that advisory the POL-RD-03 defence can be tested and the case decided — on the figures above if it fails, or on exoneration with the off-route ERP pass-through treatment in POL-RD-09(2) if it succeeds."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}

